module basics.tuple_forward_component

// A structural pair whose COMPONENT TYPE is declared BELOW it in the file, and
// declared `value` so the component is embedded rather than pointed at.
//
// What this pins: the typedef of the pair must be written after `Inner`'s body
// is complete. Until 2026-09-04 that held by CALL ORDER -- a door of its own,
// invoked right after the declaration pass -- and from V3-3 it holds because
// the structural terms are the declaration pass's last step. The carrier does
// not care which of the two it is; it fails the moment neither is true.
//
// Honest about what it does NOT cover, because the measurement said so: the
// shape where the order could actually go wrong is a DECLARED type embedding a
// pair by value, and the subset refuses a pair as a field type by name
// ("this type form is not compiled yet"). So no carrier can reach that today,
// and this one pins the direction that is reachable.

fn main() {
    ro i = Inner { v: 7 }
    ro p = (i, 2)
    println(p.1)
}

type Inner value {
    ro v int
}
